The rules with precedences, verified:

```js
// RULE 3 (implicit): the RECEIVER — obj.method(): this = obj
const user = { name: 'Al', getName() { return this.name; } };
user.getName();   // 'Al' ✓ — called ON user

// THE DETACH BUG — extract the method, the receiver is lost
const ref = user.getName;
ref();             // undefined — plain call = RULE 4 (default this)
// this is the #1 real-world this-bug: callbacks detach receivers
// (setTimeout(obj.method) / arr.forEach(obj.method) — same bug)
// FIX: bind it — obj.method.bind(obj), or arrow: () => obj.method()

// RULE 2 (explicit): call / apply / bind
fn.call(ctx, a, b);       // invoke NOW with this=ctx, args listed
fn.apply(ctx, [a, b]);    // same, args as array
const bound = fn.bind(ctx);  // a NEW function with this LOCKED

// RULE 1 (new): this = the freshly created object
function Person(name) { this.name = name; }   // this = the new instance
new Person('Al').name;   // 'Al'

// RULE 5 (arrows): NO this of their own — capture the enclosing one
const obj = {
  name: 'Outer',
  regular() { return this.name; },     // 'Outer' — implicit rule ✓
  arrow: () => this?.name,             // undefined — module-scope this
  delay() {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(this.name), 100);  // 'Outer' — the
  }  // arrow captured the METHOD's this (the receiver)
};
```

The precedence order (how to READ any this): new beats explicit beats implicit beats default; arrows sit OUTSIDE the rules — they don't get bound, they carry the captured one through everything (bind on an arrow changes nothing this-related).

The detach fix patterns (what the question is really about): bind at extraction (obj.method.bind(obj)), wrap in an arrow (() => obj.method()), pass a closure to the callback API, or make the method an arrow class-field (React class components' handleX = () => {} — pre-bound forever).

The binding rules, verified:

```js
// 1. Method call — this = the object before the dot
const obj = {
  name: 'Al',
  greet() { return this.name },  // this = obj
};
console.log(obj.greet()); // 'Al'

// 2. Plain call — this = undefined (strict) or global (sloppy)
function plain() { return this }
console.log(plain()); // undefined in modules / window in browser sloppy

// 3. Arrow function — NO own this — inherits from the creation scope
const obj2 = {
  name: 'Bo',
  greet: () => 'arrow: ' + this.name, // this = the enclosing scope (NOT obj2)
};
console.log(obj2.greet()); // 'arrow: undefined' — the arrow doesn't see obj2

// 4. Explicit binding — call/apply/bind
function introduce(greeting) { return greeting + ', ' + this.name }
const person = { name: 'Cy' };
console.log(introduce.call(person, 'Hi'));  // 'Hi, Cy' — this = person
console.log(introduce.bind(person)('Hey')); // 'Hey, Cy' — permanently bound
```

The four rules (in precedence order): NEW binding (constructor call) > explicit (bind/call/apply) > method call (obj.fn) > default (undefined/global). Arrow functions BYPASS all four — they capture this at creation and never change.

The arrow-vs-method distinction (the interview's core): a METHOD needs this of the object — use method shorthand. A CALLBACK inside a method needs the OUTER this — use an arrow. The classic bug: setTimeout in a method, using a regular function — this is lost (undefined), because the callback runs as a plain call. The arrow fixes it: setTimeout(() => this.save(), 1000) — the arrow inherits the method's this.

bind's permanence: bind returns a NEW function with this permanently bound — the bound function's this can't be re-bound (bind-ing again binds args, not this). The use: pre-bound methods as event handlers (const handler = this.handleClick.bind(this)).
